WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
(upbeat music)

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:04.410
- [Narrator] Land west of
Phoenix looked like this in 2002.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:06.840
Now it's covered in buildings.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.820
Phoenix, along with Las Vegas,

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:12.090
is experiencing a stretch
of triple digit temperatures

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:14.010
made worse by a heat dome.

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:15.300
- [Reporter] Record level temperatures

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.070
shows no signs of letting up.

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:18.870
- [Reporter] Unrelenting
grip on the country.

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:21.180
- A lot of our cities have gotten bigger

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:23.370
in the last 20 years,
especially in the southwest.

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:24.240
- [Narrator] Here's what happens

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.250
when heat domes form above cities

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:29.793
and how urban growth can make
that heat even more deadly.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.330
In Phoenix, over 20 days in July

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:36.300
have been at or above 110 degrees.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.460
And space technology company, Maxar

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:39.960
which has been tracking the weather

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:41.970
predicts the city will hit at least 30

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:43.620
by the end of the month.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:47.850
In Las Vegas, 16 days are
predicted to hit that high.

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:51.390
That heat is driven in
part by urban development.

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:55.500
- Imagine a city as an
island with pavement, roads,

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:58.590
surfaces, glass and steel buildings.

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:01.380
All of these surfaces reflect sunlight

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:05.190
and that heat energy from the
sun back into the air around.

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:06.750
- [Narrator] These highly developed areas

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:10.650
can see midday temperatures
that are 15 to 20 degrees warmer

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:13.410
than surrounding vegetated areas.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.360
We can see how these heat islands formed

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.070
by looking back at Phoenix.

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:19.380
Here it is 23 years ago

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:21.390
but after two decades of urban growth

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:22.953
the city has ballooned.

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:27.840
Back in 2000, Las Vegas looked like this

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.393
and now its city limits have expanded.

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:34.170
That expansion paralleled
their economic growth

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:36.690
but more infrastructure
turned up the heat.

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:40.230
- These are all places where
heat gets trapped, right?

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:43.800
So in many of these cities,
there's not a lot of breezes.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:47.640
There's not a lot of shade
or trees, and so as a result

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.580
heat can build up in
these urban corridors.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:54.690
- It's sometimes hard to
grow trees in the west

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:57.150
especially in very arid places

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:58.860
and the native trees that grow there

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.290
may not be great at providing shade.

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:05.163
So you have a lot of areas
that get a lot of sun.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:09.390
- The other important thing
about plants and trees

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:11.190
and vegetation and even grass

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:14.730
is they cycle moisture from
the ground into the air

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:18.270
and help just a little
bit, keep things cooler.

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:19.860
- [Narrator] That's
driven Thompson, Phoenix

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:22.140
and Las Vegas even higher

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:24.690
and drove the creation
of these heat islands.

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:27.930
But that's not the only thing
fueling the hot weather.

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:30.690
- When you have a heat dome on top of this

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:33.420
it really intensifies the temperatures

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.670
for everyone living in this city.

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:38.220
- [Narrator] These domes are
a natural weather phenomenon

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:42.000
and for most of July, one has
been covering the southwest.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.540
- A heat dome is a large
ridge of high pressure

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:48.390
high in the atmosphere,
that basically traps

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:50.520
a lot of hot air underneath it

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:52.680
and it can last for a long time.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:56.910
Over time, the heat dome is
basically reinforcing itself

00:02:56.910 --> 00:03:01.140
in the sense that any moisture
on the ground or in the soil

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:03.420
that may come up into the atmosphere

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:04.740
that perhaps could cause some

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:07.020
rain or rainstorms in the afternoon

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:07.950
that's going away.

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:11.250
The soils are drying out,
the land is drying out

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:14.460
and so this hot air is making
things worse and worse.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:15.930
- [Narrator] The
combination of a heat dome

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:17.580
on top of a heat island

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:21.120
means that being outside
can be even more dangerous.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:22.890
Burn centers have reported injuries

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:24.960
to people who touch hot door handles

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:27.660
and walk barefoot on scorched pavement.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:29.520
- These are hot places, right?

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:31.290
And so there's often this feeling of

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:32.550
well, it's always been hot here

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:34.320
we're used to it, we can deal with it.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:37.680
But I think that masks some of
the really significant risks

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:40.980
that are faced by people
experiencing homelessness

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:43.830
by people who are elderly,
people who work outside.

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:45.240
Many of these different groups

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.500
that are exposed and vulnerable

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:50.880
- [Reporter] In cities
already experiencing

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:52.560
triple digit temperatures

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.710
The National Oceanic and
Atmospheric Administration

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:58.920
is preparing officials for
increasing heat events.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:01.980
- Everybody is very
concerned about the future

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:04.710
that with warming and with climate change

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:06.870
we expect more of these events to happen.

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:09.600
And when these events
happen, they're more intense.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:12.570
- [Narrator] And scientists
say the heat is here to stay.

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:15.630
- As architects, planners, and
engineers look to the future

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:17.040
they have to take into account

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.350
the effects of climate change over time

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:20.880
temperatures that are rising

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:23.700
storms and other things
that are happening as well.

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:25.560
So they're taking these
things into account

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:27.450
with new kinds of technologies,

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:30.570
green roofs that can
absorb and cool buildings

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:34.140
and also just in the design
and shape of buildings

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:37.350
that can provide more
shade and maybe just have

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:40.410
a little bit of resiliency
toward a warming climate

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:42.167
that we're all facing in the future.

00:04:42.167 --> 00:04:44.750
(upbeat music)

